              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null. The code copies a string literal into a destination buffer using a function that does not check for null pointers. There is no evidence of a guard or check to ensure that the destination pointer is valid before the operation. The source is a string literal, which is always valid, but the destination may be null, as there is no explicit check in the code. This can lead to a crash or other unpredictable behavior if the destination pointer is null at runtime.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior if the destination is null. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (status && status->card_model) {\n    strncpy(status->card_model, \"0x0b0c\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->card_model[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis prevents the function from attempting to write to a null pointer and also ensures the destination string is null-terminated, which is a common pitfall with strncpy.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3ac63cc4-461d-3272-b5fe-d4d5aa11f114",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null, and there are no obvious null pointer checks before the buffer operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', confirming the high confidence in the issue. The source is a string literal, which is always valid, but the destination pointer may be null, as there is no evidence of a guard or check to ensure it is non-null. This can lead to a crash or undefined behavior if the function is called with a null destination pointer. The probability of this being a false positive is very low due to the lack of null checks and the explicit risk classification.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the buffer copy operation, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. This prevents undefined behavior and potential crashes if the function is called with a null pointer. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (status && status->card_series) {\n    strncpy(status->card_series, \"AMD INSTINCT MI200 (MCM) OAM AC MBA MSFT\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination:\n    status->card_series[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis check ensures that both the structure and the destination buffer are valid before copying the string. Also, consider explicitly null-terminating the destination buffer after using `strncpy` to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a6ac8cb8-43fc-3b71-a13f-d0ee51049394",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null, and there are no obvious null pointer checks before the buffer operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', confirming the high confidence in the issue. The source is a string literal, which is always valid, but the destination pointer may be null, as there is no evidence of a guard or check to ensure it is not null before use. This could lead to a crash or other undefined behavior if the destination pointer is ever null at runtime. The absence of a null-terminator assignment after the call further increases the risk, as strncpy does not guarantee null-termination if the source string length equals or exceeds the specified count. The probability of this being a false positive is very low due to these factors.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data into the destination buffer, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add an explicit check for the destination pointer and handle the error appropriately. Also, ensure that the buffer is null-terminated after the copy, as `strncpy` does not guarantee null-termination if the source string is as long as or longer than the specified count.\n\n```c++\nif (status && status->serial_num) {\n    strncpy(status->serial_num, \"PCB046982-0071\", AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->serial_num[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle error: status or serial_num is null\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cd7b2a2a-fae8-38df-986d-eaa52c70c3e8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination pointers are non-null, which further increases the risk of runtime errors such as segmentation faults. The absence of explicit null-termination after the copy operation also means that the destination buffer may not be properly terminated, potentially leading to string handling errors. These factors indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to ensure program correctness and security.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the string copy operation, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. Additionally, add explicit null pointer checks for both the source and destination pointers to prevent segmentation faults. After copying, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer to avoid unterminated strings.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && block && fw_version->firmware != block) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->firmware, block, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1);\n    fw_version->firmware[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n- This code checks that both pointers are non-null and that the source and destination do not overlap.\n- It copies up to one less than the maximum length to leave space for the null terminator.\n- It explicitly sets the last byte of the destination buffer to the null character to ensure proper string termination.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d831dc05-dce8-36c3-aeb1-1f6aaa3249a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination pointers are non-null, which could lead to null pointer dereference issues. The absence of explicit null-termination after the copy further increases the risk of string-related bugs. These factors indicate a real risk that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the string copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the pointers are not null and that the regions are distinct. Also, ensure the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after the copy to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && version &&\n    ((const void *)fw_version->version + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN <= (const void *)version ||\n     (const void *)version + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN <= (const void *)fw_version->version)) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, version, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis code checks for null pointers and ensures that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. It also explicitly null-terminates the destination buffer.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bbc7801f-f14f-367b-b616-64e47d57a956",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that arises when a program uses a function that can lead to a security risk. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which converts a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not perform any error checking. If the input string cannot be converted to an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use functions that perform error checking. In C++, you can use the `std::stoi()` function, which throws an exception if the conversion fails. Always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `std::stoi()`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\ntry {\n    number_of_threads = std::stoi(argv[1]);\n} catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {\n    // Handle exception\n} catch (std::out_of_range& e) {\n    // Handle exception\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<string>`: This library is required for `std::stoi()`.\n- `<cstdlib>`: This library is required for `std::invalid_argument` and `std::out_of_range`.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6749197c-f15e-3cc5-8fdb-635dcb4274f3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nAn \"Unsecured Web Address\" vulnerability refers to the use of unencrypted HTTP protocol to communicate sensitive information. This can lead to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks where an attacker can intercept and possibly alter the communication between two parties without their knowledge. In the context of C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a program uses an unsecured HTTP address to send or receive sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use secure protocols such as HTTPS for communication, especially when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS ensures that the data is encrypted and secure from eavesdropping or tampering.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are using a library like libcurl to make HTTP requests, you can simply replace the \"http://\" in the URL with \"https://\". For example, if the vulnerable code is:\n\n```cpp\n#include <curl/curl.h>\n\nint main() {\n    CURL *curl;\n    CURLcode res;\n\n    curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_DEFAULT);\n\n    curl = curl_easy_init();\n    if(curl) {\n        curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, \"http://bugs.opensolaris.org\");\n        /* Perform the request, res will get the return code */ \n        res = curl_easy_perform(curl);\n        /* Check for errors */ \n        if(res != CURLE_OK)\n            fprintf(stderr, \"curl_easy_perform() failed: %s\\n\", curl_easy_strerror(res));\n\n        /* always cleanup */ \n        curl_easy_cleanup(curl);\n    }\n\n    curl_global_cleanup();\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nYou can fix it by changing the URL to use HTTPS:\n\n```cpp\ncurl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, \"https://bugs.opensolaris.org\");\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the libcurl library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1ae5108e-ce58-3fa6-9b6d-a4812b36c401",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including denial of service, data corruption, and arbitrary code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use safer string copy functions that include array bounds checking, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `snprintf`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than the specified number of characters are copied to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string source = name + std::to_string(gpu1->id());\nstrncpy(info->peer_device[walk_ctxt->count].peer_device.name, source.c_str(), sizeof(info->peer_device[walk_ctxt->count].peer_device.name) - 1);\ninfo->peer_device[walk_ctxt->count].peer_device.name[sizeof(info->peer_device[walk_ctxt->count].peer_device.name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIn this fix, `strncpy` is used instead of `strcpy`, and the size of the destination buffer is passed as the third argument to ensure that the function does not write past the end of the buffer. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated, as `strncpy` does not null-terminate the string if the source string is longer than the specified length.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `strcpy` and `strncpy`\n- `<string>` for `std::to_string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "45b23a69-1881-3295-bc69-5c7a3eaf6efe",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the C standard for strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of a guard for buffer boundaries and the lack of explicit null-termination further increase the risk. These factors indicate a real and actionable security or correctness issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the strncpy operation, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a temporary buffer to safely copy the data. Additionally, add explicit null pointer checks for both source and destination pointers to prevent null dereference. Finally, ensure the destination buffer is null-terminated after the copy to avoid unterminated strings.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (status && vbios_info.part_number && status->vbios_part_number != vbios_info.part_number) {\n    char temp[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN];\n    strncpy(temp, vbios_info.part_number, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1);\n    temp[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n    strncpy(status->vbios_part_number, temp, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    status->vbios_part_number[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis approach ensures that the source and destination do not overlap, prevents null pointer dereference, and guarantees null-termination of the destination buffer.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9b4961b6-2dce-3810-967d-d2029f8082e5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the strncpy function and can lead to unpredictable results or program crashes. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination pointers are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of a guard for buffer sizes and the lack of explicit null-termination after the copy further increase the risk. These factors indicate a real and actionable security or correctness issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the strncpy operation, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a function that safely handles overlapping memory, such as memmove, but only if the data is not a string or if you can guarantee null-termination. Additionally, always check that both source and destination pointers are non-null before copying, and explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after the copy to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n**Example Fix:**\n\n```cpp\nif (status && vbios_info.version && status->vbios_version != vbios_info.version) {\n    strncpy(status->vbios_version, vbios_info.version, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1);\n    status->vbios_version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis fix adds null pointer checks, ensures the source and destination do not overlap, copies at most one less than the buffer size, and explicitly null-terminates the destination buffer.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "799588e8-8120-3b3d-b263-af62f597af9f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination pointers are non-null, which could lead to null pointer dereference. The absence of explicit bounds checks or null-termination further increases the risk. The verdict is supported by the classification and the fact that the analysis determined overlap feasibility through data flow analysis, and both source and destination pointers may be null.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is safe for overlapping regions. Also, add explicit null pointer checks for both source and destination pointers to prevent null dereference. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (status && driver_info.driver_version && status->driver_version != driver_info.driver_version) {\n    memmove(status->driver_version, driver_info.driver_version, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that the operation is safe even if the memory regions overlap and that neither pointer is null before the operation.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [CWE-124: Buffer Underwrite ('Buffer Underflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/124.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
