            {
              "id": "e625a29e-e0ba-3b4f-a4e1-b6d216db1154",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null. The code copies a string representation of a version number into a destination buffer using a function that does not guarantee null-termination if the buffer size is reached. There is no evidence of a guard or check to ensure that the destination pointer is not null before the copy operation. The source is a string generated at runtime, but the main risk is the lack of validation for the destination pointer, which could lead to a crash or undefined behavior if it is null. The absence of a null-terminator assignment after the copy further increases the risk of string-related vulnerabilities. The analysis also notes that the destination pointer may be null, and there are no guards in place to prevent this. Therefore, this is a genuine issue that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Also, after using the copy function, explicitly set the last character of the buffer to a null terminator to guarantee proper string termination. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && fw_version->version) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, std::to_string(version).c_str(), AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference and ensures the string is always null-terminated, reducing the risk of buffer overflows and string-related vulnerabilities.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "13389990-0718-3746-8363-c80d117ed313",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the data flow analysis. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination pointers are non-null, which increases the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of explicit buffer size information and lack of a null-terminator assignment after the copy further increase the risk. These factors together make this a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nBefore performing the string copy, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the pointers are not null and that the memory regions are distinct. Also, ensure the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after the copy to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && buf && fw_version->version != buf) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach checks for null pointers and ensures the source and destination are not the same. It also explicitly null-terminates the destination buffer to avoid unterminated string issues.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nIf there is a possibility that the source and destination buffers may overlap, consider using a safer copy function that handles overlapping memory regions, such as `memmove`, but only if the data being copied is not a string or does not require null-termination. For string data, ensure you handle null-termination manually.\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && buf && fw_version->version != buf) {\n    memmove(fw_version->version, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nNote: Use this approach only if you are certain that the data being copied does not require strict string semantics, as `memmove` does not guarantee null-termination.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
