            {
              "id": "647724da-3b6f-3998-8fa6-9857a7e6afa4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The source and destination expressions are related by data flow, which means they may refer to overlapping memory. This is dangerous because strncpy does not guarantee correct behavior when source and destination overlap, potentially leading to data corruption or unpredictable results. Additionally, there is no evidence of checks to ensure that the source and destination are not null, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. The explicit assignment of a null terminator after the call does not mitigate the overlap risk. The verdict is supported by the classification and the high-confidence 'GENUINE' label from the analysis.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the risk of undefined behavior when source and destination memory regions may overlap, ensure that the source and destination do not overlap before calling `strncpy`. If overlap is possible, use a memory-safe function such as `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. For example:\n\n```cpp\n// Only use strncpy if you are certain src and dst do not overlap.\nif (!regions_overlap(event_record.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR)) {\n    strncpy(event_record.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n    event_record.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    memmove(event_record.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n    event_record.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n\n// Helper function to check for overlap (implement as needed)\nbool regions_overlap(const void* dst, const void* src, size_t len) {\n    const char* d = (const char*)dst;\n    const char* s = (const char*)src;\n    return (d < s + len) && (s < d + len);\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you know that overlap is not possible by design, document this assumption clearly and add assertions to enforce it at runtime:\n\n```cpp\nassert(!regions_overlap(event_record.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR));\nstrncpy(event_record.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\nevent_record.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
